permalink: /sitemap.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#- The sitemap. Driven by collections.all, so a page added later appears here
    without anyone remembering to come back.

    IT LISTED ONE URL UNTIL 2026-08-29, AND THAT WAS CORRECT. It now lists 48: the
    homepage and the 47 documentation pages (D-008). Docs are meant to be found —
    a technical buyer searching for a field name is exactly the reader they are for
    — so unlike privacy they are a positive request to index.

    Both non-docs pages set `eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true` and neither
    belongs in a sitemap:

    · /404.html is not content.

    · /privacy/ is deliberately unlinked — Nandan, 2026-08-25: it "exists at its URL
      for anyone who needs it." A sitemap entry is a positive request to index, which
      is the opposite of that intent. It carries no noindex, so anyone who has the URL
      still gets the page; it simply is not advertised. If the policy is ever linked
      from the page, revisit this.

    Not an Eleventy .html page (D-006's "pages are .html with front matter and a
    layout") because it is not a page — it is a machine artefact that happens to be
    generated. eleventy.config.js adds "njk" to templateFormats for this file and
    for docs-search-index.njk, which is a machine artefact on the same terms. -#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for p in collections.all %}
  <url>
    <loc>{{ site.url }}{{ p.url }}</loc>
    <lastmod>{{ p.date.toISOString() }}</lastmod>
  </url>
{%- endfor %}
</urlset>
